---
import NeoVimLayout from '../layouts/NeoVimLayout.astro';

let lineNr = 1;
---

<NeoVimLayout title="About" filename="about.ts" filetype="typescript">
  <article class="code about" aria-label="About Vaibhav">
    <div class="code-line anim-line" style="--i: 0">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">// about.ts</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line anim-line" style="--i: 1">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line anim-line" style="--i: 2">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">/**</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 3">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>Hi, I’m Vaibhav. I’ve been working as a software engineer for a little over four years now. I started by building geospatial software in the drone industry and later moved to a very early stage SaaS startup. Somewhere along the way, I became increasingly curious about what happens lower down the stack, especially inside databases.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__spacer anim-line" style="--i: 4">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 5">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>These days, I’m trying to understand that world by reimplementing parts of SQLite in Rust. The project is called <a href="https://github.com/vaibhav135/toydb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>toydb</code></a>. So far, it can read SQLite’s record format, walk through table and index B trees, including both interior pages and leaf pages, follow overflow page chains, and run full table scans and a small set of <code>SELECT</code> queries, including simple equality lookups.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__spacer anim-line" style="--i: 6">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 7">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>There are still plenty of gaps. The parser and query executor are fairly small, and I haven’t tackled <code>WITHOUT ROWID</code> tables yet. I found them a little weird at first. They are tables, but their rows are stored in an index B tree, and they can still have secondary indexes of their own. It is interesting, but also a rabbit hole I decided to save for another day.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__spacer anim-line" style="--i: 8">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 9">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>Next, I want to build a proper SQL parser instead of continuing to stretch the small one I have now. I’m currently working through <a href="https://craftinginterpreters.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><cite>Crafting Interpreters</cite></a> to understand parsing and AST construction properly before bringing those ideas back into <code>toydb</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__spacer anim-line" style="--i: 10">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 11">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>When I’m not staring at SQLite page layouts, I’m usually listening to Indian classical or Carnatic music. That’s basically my jam. I have also been running 6 km every morning because I’m trying to lose some weight. So far, that has been a pretty good motivation to keep going.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__spacer anim-line" style="--i: 12">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line about__paragraph anim-line" style="--i: 13">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <p class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">* </span>I’m currently open to systems and database engineering roles where I can keep learning and work on problems closer to the internals. If you think I could be a good fit for your team, feel free to <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="code-line anim-line" style="--i: 14">
      <span class="line-nr">{lineNr++}</span>
      <span class="line-content"><span class="syn-comment">*/</span><span class="cursor-block"></span></span>
    </div>
  </article>
</NeoVimLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .about__paragraph {
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding-block: 0.35rem;
  }

  .about__paragraph .line-content {
    max-width: 86ch;
    margin: 0;
    white-space: normal;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .about__spacer {
    min-height: 0.75rem;
  }

  .about a {
    color: var(--green);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--comment);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .about a:hover {
    color: var(--cyan);
    text-decoration-color: var(--cyan);
  }

  .about code {
    color: var(--orange);
    font-family: inherit;
  }

  .about cite {
    color: var(--yellow);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .about__paragraph .line-content {
      line-height: 1.65;
    }
  }
</style>
